{
  "$comment": "FROZEN designed surface (covenant). This document, not the code, is what the CLI and MCP surface is: `kernel/spec/surfaceConformance.ts` asserts the emitted surface against it in BOTH directions, so a verb, flag, argument shape or tool name that is not written here cannot ship, and one written here cannot silently disappear. Every entry below is a published contract; editing one is a deliberate covenant change that moves the emitters, the tests and the docs in the same commit, and it costs a CHANGELOG row. The fixed sections mirror FIXED_SURFACE in kernel/spec/emitSurface.ts verbatim. Prompt NAMES are graph data, guarded by the prompt tests, not frozen here. `mcpSurface.resources` is PROTOCOL IDENTITY and is frozen DELIBERATELY, not by omission: the scheme is inherited unchanged by a fork, together with the `<scheme>/box` and `<scheme>/instanceCount` `_meta` keys namespaced under it, because a client that has stored a resource URI has stored an address. Deriving it per distribution is the defect this clause exists to prevent — an attempt that derived the TEMPLATE while leaving the two minting sites literal made all 653 of a fork's advertised resources unreadable, and the readable form was never advertised. `capabilities/resources/resources.test.ts` pins all four writings (this entry, the provider's declared template, every minted URI, and both `_meta` namespaces) to one scheme token, so the template and the minting sites can only move together; and because all four are the same token under a distribution actually named `pragma`, `src/identity.test.ts` runs those same four checks under a FORK's name, against this entry, where a DERIVED template would diverge.",
